WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.630
PROFESSOR DAVID WEITZ:
All fluids have viscosity.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:09.860
Viscosity is the resistance
of a fluid to flow.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.280
The more the resistance,
the higher the viscosity.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.020
Think of walking through water.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.320
As you walk through water,
the water puts a force on you

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.180
and prevents you from
walking through it.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.940
But now, think about
walking through honey.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:28.100
It's even more difficult. Honey is
much more viscous than water is,

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.040
and it's much more difficult
to walk through honey

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.930
than it is to walk through water.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:38.870
The fluid puts a force, or a
stress, that prevents the flow.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:42.950
The stress is the viscosity
times the rate of flow,

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.350
or the rate at which the
fluid flows over itself.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.670
Here's the formula for the viscosity.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:54.030
The units are stress times
time, or pascal seconds.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.060
And there are many culinary examples.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:00.860
The simplest, of course, is water,
which flows much more easily than oil.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.650
Water is much less viscous than oil.

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:09.690
But oil has a viscosity that changes
very strongly with temperature.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.350
If you increase the temperature,
the viscosity of oil

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.630
decreases quite substantially.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:19.640
In fact, if you heat it up enough,
it can become as low viscosity

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.710
as is water.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:26.660
Many fluids have a very strongly
temperature-dependent viscosity,

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.570
and we use that in cooking very often.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.810
To understand viscosity
from a more molecular view,

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.880
let's look at this little cartoon.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:38.100
The fluid is made up of
many, many molecules,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.910
and the molecules are
constantly moving around.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:43.340
But in order to cause
the fluid to flow--

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:48.990
like this-- we have to cause the
molecules to move over one another.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:52.100
In fact, for the fluid to
actually flow, the molecules

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.010
have to exchange places with each other.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:02:00.690
And so the viscosity depends on how
fast they change places with each other.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.610
So the viscosity is given by
the density times the speed

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:07.010
of the molecules times their size.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:12.090
It's just a measure of how fast the
molecules move over one another.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.200
And you can see immediately
that larger molecules

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:20.230
take longer because they have a longer
way to go to move over one another.

